JIT slow paths into cold chunks, on by default (EDEN_JIT_COLD=0 turns them off).
A block's deferred emits (fastmem/page-table fallbacks, FP NaN and saturation paths) were written
right after its hot code, so hot code was diluted and scattered: on the PS5 lab, OOT's critical
cores ran JIT code at IPC 0.64 with 44% of cycles starved for instructions, only 26% of uops from
the op cache, and half their JIT time spread over 581 KiB of 65%-used lines on 1,005 pages
(coordination/notes/lab-frontend.md). With the switch on, slow paths go into 256 KiB chunks
reserved at the hot frontier, and hot code continues after each chunk. Committed memory still grows
in order (a chunk is committed when reserved). Only with a separate writable view (cold code is
written behind the frontier). A block's registered size is its hot part. ClearCache resets the
chunk (headless/jit-permissions.inc). EdenJitColdOverride() lets a test driver flip it together with a
full JIT clear (live A/B in one session).
Measured: lab OOT ABBA 33.1/33.2 -> 35.1/35.6 fps; Pro OOT (pinned at 30 fps) cores 0-2 busy 67.0/65.1% -> 61.1/61.4%.
